y by Hilton, Graduate by Hilton, Tempo by Hilton, Motto by Hilton, Hilton Hotels & Resorts, DoubleTree by Hilton, Curio Collection by Hilton, Tapestry Collection by Hilton, Outset Collection by Hilton, Embassy Suites by Hilton, Homewood Suites by Hilton, Home2 Suites by Hilton, LivSmart Studios by Hilton, Hilton Garden Inn, Hampton by Hilton, Tru by Hilton, Spark by Hilton, Hilton Grand Vacations, Small Luxury Hotels of the World, AutoCamp, and Hilton Honors brand names. The company has operations in North America, South America, and Central America, including various Caribbean nations; Europe, the Middle East, and Africa; and the Asia Pacific. Hilton Worldwide Holdings Inc. was founded in 1919 and is headquartered in McLean, Virginia.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Christopher J. Nassetta | President, CEO & Director | Age 62 | Compensation: $4.6M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 182K people. Revenue per employee of $66K ranks in the bottom quartile among Consumer Cyclical peers. Each employee generates $11K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $934 per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 69\u00d7 revenue per employee (elevated relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags elevated compensation governance risk (7/10).","financial_summary":["Hilton Worldwide Holdings Inc. is a large-cap consumer cyclical business with a $79.3B market cap on $12.0B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +11% over the past three years and +1% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 24% against a 9% cost of capital \u2014 a 15-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat.","The business throws off $2.0B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 17 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 1.46\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is moderate at 4.0\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, serviceable in normal conditions but worth watching through a downturn, and interest coverage runs at 4.3\u00d7.","Shareholder yield of 4.2% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insiders hold 2.2% of the shares, and within its sector the business captures 2.8\u00d7 its fair share of profits, a structural advantage visible in the numbers."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 140% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":1.402801719756655},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $62.0M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Cyclical sectors like Consumer Cyclical tend to underperform in slowdowns","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Consumer staples outperforming discretionary, signaling cautious spending","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"High market volatility with VIX at 36, signaling elevated fear","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Widening credit spreads suggest growing stress in corporate debt markets","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Broad market in a downtrend, trading below its 200-day average","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Brand relevance decay \u2014 shifting consumer preferences and social-media-driven trends can strand inventory and invalidate positioning faster than product cycles can adapt.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Operating margins 15% above sector median \u2014 the quantitative trace of pricin